 ### Decided at the panel (RULINGs 15-19), with the cost of each
 
 - **Q2, a gauge with no data draws no overlay (RULING 15).** The live *The Fuel Cell Shows the Served Fuel Tonnage and Estimated Range Over a Gauge* takes no delta. Cost: a dry fleet that selects a target sees no overlay; its fuel figure reads absent and the command panel says "Not affordable with the fuel aboard", so only the panel's text carries its verdict. The other option would have drawn the cost on an empty rail and forced a MODIFIED block of four scenarios.
-- **Q4, paint and the non-colour cue (RULING 16).** The extent is a partial-height band drawn separately from the fill, with no minimum drawn width and no change to the rail; the unaffordable state adds the fixed-size full-height mark; exact classes are `design.md`'s. Paint legibility is judged at the journey as a measurement (the sampled centre pixels of the fill, the extent and the rail are pairwise unequal sRGB values in the scheme rendered) plus a tier-1 class-family assertion. Tier 1 asserts the mark's position (start equals the extent's length in percent, pull-back equals the same percentage of its own width, at costs 0, 100 and 250 t, so a mark with no pull-back fails), its non-zero fixed size and full height, the band's part height, and that the mark's colour tokens differ from the unaffordable band's. Cost: the contrast ratios are recorded and not gated, because the reviewer's figures are hand-computed and no zinc shade already on the gauge was found to meet both surfaces in the dark scheme; the extent-past-fill arrangement and the mark's own sampled paint are judged only if the journey arranges them, else recorded untested; what no tier observes is the mark's pixel containment (jsdom does no layout) and the rounded cap's clipping at the rail's ends.
